WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.940
Why does a yellow mouse never breed true?

2
00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:11.060
Mate two yellow mice again and again, and black pups keep turning up.

3
00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:14.120
One litter may be all yellow by chance.

4
00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:17.360
But no yellow line ever stays yellow.

5
00:00:18.433 --> 00:00:20.833
That looked like a failure of Mendel's rules.

6
00:00:22.173 --> 00:00:26.873
It was not. It was a clue that inheritance works in two layers.

7
00:00:28.017 --> 00:00:29.697
The first layer is the deal.

8
00:00:30.957 --> 00:00:34.937
At meiosis, each parent's two alleles separate into different gametes.

9
00:00:36.017 --> 00:00:38.417
So each child gets one allele from each parent.

10
00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:43.880
Cross two parents that carry the same two alleles, one copy of each.

11
00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:49.860
Each parent passes on either allele equally often, and gametes meet at random.

12
00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:53.560
So the pairings fill four equally likely boxes.

13
00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:56.780
One box gets two of the first allele.

14
00:00:58.100 --> 00:00:59.560
Two boxes get one of each.

15
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:02.380
One box gets two of the second.

16
00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:05.670
That deal is the part Mendel got right.

17
00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:08.610
It never changes in this chapter.

18
00:01:09.910 --> 00:01:12.390
What changes is the label written in each box.

19
00:01:13.533 --> 00:01:16.413
In Mendel's peas, one purple allele is enough.

20
00:01:17.693 --> 00:01:21.393
A plant with one purple allele is as purple as a plant with two.

21
00:01:22.653 --> 00:01:24.473
So three boxes share one label.

22
00:01:25.673 --> 00:01:27.633
That is Mendel's familiar ratio.

23
00:01:28.717 --> 00:01:30.937
Snapdragons write the labels differently.

24
00:01:32.277 --> 00:01:34.677
Cross red with white, and every child is pink.

25
00:01:36.057 --> 00:01:39.437
In a snapdragon, one red allele gives only a pink flower.

26
00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:42.970
Now let the pink plants pollinate themselves.

27
00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:45.510
Red and white come back.

28
00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:48.210
Each box keeps its own label.

29
00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:50.550
Nothing blended.

30
00:01:51.483 --> 00:01:52.923
Now the yellow mouse.

31
00:01:54.223 --> 00:01:56.923
One yellow allele is enough to make the coat yellow.

32
00:01:58.423 --> 00:02:01.263
Mate two yellow mice, and the same four boxes appear.

33
00:02:02.367 --> 00:02:05.267
So what label goes in the box with two yellow alleles?

34
00:02:11.683 --> 00:02:13.063
That label is death.

35
00:02:14.303 --> 00:02:19.183
Two copies of the yellow allele kill the embryo very early, before implantation.

36
00:02:20.443 --> 00:02:23.063
So that box goes dark and drops out of the count.

37
00:02:24.117 --> 00:02:28.477
So among live pups, yellow outnumbers black by the ratio on screen.

38
00:02:29.857 --> 00:02:34.297
And every yellow pup is a heterozygote, with one yellow allele and one other.

39
00:02:35.367 --> 00:02:37.687
That is why no yellow mouse breeds true.

40
00:02:39.067 --> 00:02:43.407
Two heterozygotes always deal some boxes with no yellow allele at all.

41
00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:45.920
So here is the idea.

42
00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:48.700
Inheritance deals the boxes.

43
00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:52.440
What each allele's product does writes the labels.

44
00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:58.240
Some boxes share a label, some keep their own, and one can be labelled dies.

45
00:02:59.283 --> 00:03:04.403
This holds while the deal is fair, the genes are unlinked, and each box gets one

46
00:03:04.403 --> 00:03:09.463
label. When a genotype gives only a chance of a trait, the rule stops.
